# Line endings.
#
# Git is left to its own devices for most files, but anything executed by a
# Linux kernel must be LF regardless of the platform it was checked out on.
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the Windows default, and a shell script checked out
# with CRLF ships a shebang the kernel reads as the literal command `bash\r`:
#
#     env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# which is what a deployment from a Windows machine failed with. The error
# names neither the file nor the line ending, so it is worth preventing rather
# than diagnosing twice.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Copied into images and run by the shell there, same reasoning.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Applied with psql inside a container.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Read on the deployment VM by systemd, by Quadlet and by nginx.
#
# For the env template this matters more than for a script, and fails far more
# quietly: `podman --env-file` treats everything after the `=` as the value,
# carriage return included. DATABASE_URL then ends in a \r, Postgres is asked for
# a host that does not exist, and the error names the host rather than the
# invisible character appended to it. Every secret in the file is corrupted the
# same way, and none of them says so.
*.container text eol=lf
*.network text eol=lf
*.volume text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
.env.production.example text eol=lf
